Z||000000||BAM-BAM||11-18-2000||03:18 PM||||  I just got a new sunroof gasket for my 85GT. Hopefully this will stop the leaking. But, I also remember a post some time ago about drilling drain holes for the sunroof. Now, before I install this new gasket, is there anyone who can tell me exactly where to drill these little holes? TIA.<P> Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach Springs/KYB shocks and struts<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats, Carpet and headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Guages And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||11-18-2000||03:29 PM||||Are you absolutely sure it's the gasket that is leaking?  I was about to replace mine when Ed Parks told me that 99% of the leaky sunroofs he repairs (he's done many) are due to the rubber washers around the mounting bolts in the glass.  I replaced the mounting bolts (got them from Pontiac) with the new ones that have the integral rubber seals and the leaks stopped.<P>I've never had a plastic roof panel off to see what lies underneath but my guess is that if you drill holes in the steel channel the water will simply leak into the interior.  Where will it go otherwise?<P>My daughter's RX-7 has drain holes in each corner of the sunroof but each has a drain hose attached that leads to the ground.<P>As I said, I don't know what lies underneath but I'd be careful before I'd drill any holes.<P>Ed did mention that if the gasket isn't sealing against the glass very well to install a spacer underneath the latch mechanism.  I did not have to do that.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 11-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||11-18-2000||04:28 PM||||  Carrolles, This is where I got the idea. Also, I am very confident that the seal is bad. The water seems to leak from the rail around the sunroof. I checked the mounting hardware first, no water at all. I have also purchased all new hardware for the sunroof. So I should have all bases covered. Just as a note, I found this thread after I posted. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/003316.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/003316.html</A> <P> Peace, BAM OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> P.S. Also, it depends on the year of your Fiero; the earlier models didn't have the holes, the later models did. Not really sure when it started.<P> Late.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 11-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||11-18-2000||09:39 PM||||They go in the corners. I don't have a pic to show you. If you happen to go to a junk yard it has to be an 87 or 88 Fiero. 87 was the first year GM put the holes in. When I changed mine I drilled the holes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||11-18-2000||10:23 PM||||   Oreif, are they in the bottom most edge of the corner? I should be able to handle it.<P> Thanks, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
